        "runId": "ada-en-20261110-tsvc722k5p",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      },
      "text": "Puerto Natales, Chilean Patagonia"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_title:e1dddf681303f0bd",
      "field": "itinerary.title",
      "sourceTextSha256": "ea292aed1462936a2d3d453f4ecc918b5671af477c844c9ce3944aeb75b2fba0",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261110-tsvc722k5p",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      },
      "text": "25 December: Torres del Paine"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:b9ad3e14216c2f08",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "bfd4e379637f8f13250098b8c4593f98da162e6c01f5cccad7add49c9dc56094",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261110-tsvc722k5p",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      },
      "text": "After breakfast, visit Torres del Paine National Park with an English-speaking guide.\n\nThe park covers more than 2,000 km² and includes mountains, glaciers, waterfalls, rivers, lakes, forests and fjords. Its name refers to the three sharp granite towers that form one of Patagonia's most recognisable silhouettes. The area has been a UNESCO biosphere reserve since the late 1970s.\n\nYou may see guanacos, Andean condors, flamingos and rheas, and with luck, a puma.\n\nThe tour includes the Mylodon Cave, where remains of a sabre-toothed cat, an ancient American horse and a giant ground sloth were found. A figure of Mylodon darwini, the species the cave is named after, stands at the entrance.\n\nThe tour includes hikes of varying difficulty. One stop is Salto Grande waterfall, which links the Lake Nordenskjöld and Lake Pehoé areas. On the way back, you can see the Serrano River, Lake Toro and Lake Porteño.\n\nOvernight at the hotel in Puerto Natales."
    },
